R$\n\nIII. $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$"] ["question-5964ffcf-bc61-5cdb-8ce3-fe3d629e8c05" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $2\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] ["question-5964ffcf-bc61-5cdb-8ce3-fe3d629e8c05" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-5932e5d0-9d0c-54be-9a78-8ee0938071e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "5932e5d0-9d0c-54be-9a78-8ee0938071e4"] ["answer-a7281cf1-df65-5001-bdd3-ab6876280750" :answer/id #uuid "a7281cf1-df65-5001-bdd3-ab6876280750"] ["answer-a7281cf1-df65-5001-bdd3-ab6876280750" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-a7281cf1-df65-5001-bdd3-ab6876280750" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-e264fdd1-c875-5ee0-914c-54600fb544c3" :answer/id #uuid "e264fdd1-c875-5ee0-914c-54600fb544c3"] ["answer-e264fdd1-c875-5ee0-914c-54600fb544c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e264fdd1-c875-5ee0-914c-54600fb544c3" :answer/value "I, II, and III"] ["answer-c4208108-7b91-5e37-9f02-765baf72fbe9" :answer/id #uuid "c4208108-7b91-5e37-9f02-7